-- js-structure.rofl — the GENERIC structure over a captured AST. No domain
-- knowledge lives here: not a language's idea of a call, not a notion of
-- dataflow, not a judgement about which kinds matter. Everything below is
-- true of any tree the scanner emits, and nothing below would change if the
-- parser were pointed at another language.
--
-- Reads scanners/js_ast.ts's four relations and adds four convenience ones:
--
--   ast_in     — parent/child, forgetting which field and which position
--   ast_within — the ancestor relation, ast_in's transitive closure
--   ast_name   — the `name` attribute, under the name every rule reaches for
--   ast_value  — the `value` attribute, likewise
--
-- ast_name / ast_value are DELIBERATELY thin: they rename two attributes and
-- claim nothing about them. `name` on an Identifier and `name` on a JSX
-- attribute are the same fact here, because deciding they differ is the job
-- of the rule that cares.

-- the tree, with the field and the position forgotten
ast_in[code](P, C)     :- ast_child[code](P, _, _, C).

-- COST NOTE. ast_within is a transitive closure over the parse tree, so its
-- size is roughly nodes x depth: every node pairs with each of its ancestors.
-- On the fixtures here that is a few thousand rows and free. Before this is
-- ever pointed at a real corpus the number wants MEASURING rather than
-- assuming — a 100k-node tree at depth 30 is ~3M derived rows, and that is
-- the point where an ancestor query should be answered by walking ast_in from
-- the node rather than by materialising the closure.
ast_within[code](P, C) :- ast_in[code](P, C).
ast_within[code](P, D) :- ast_within[code](P, X), ast_in[code](X, D).

-- the two attributes worth a name of their own
ast_name[code](N, V)   :- ast_attr[code](N, name, V).

-- THE NAME A KEY STANDS FOR, wherever a key appears — an object method, an
-- object property, a class method. Written once here rather than four times
-- because it grew a second case on 2026-09-07 and the four copies would have
-- been four chances to add it three times.
--
-- A COMPUTED WELL-KNOWN SYMBOL IS A NAME. `{ [Symbol.iterator]() {} }` puts a
-- `member_expression` where every other key has an identifier, so every
-- relation reading `ast_name` on a key found nothing: the method had no name in
-- `fn_name` and no key in `member_value`, and an edge to it was derivable and
-- unreportable at once. The property is the name — `iterator` — which is what
-- V8 says with the brackets off and what this model calls every other method.
--
-- GUARDED ON `Symbol` DELIBERATELY: `obj[someVar]` has no static name and this
-- must not invent one. The well-known symbols are the only computed keys whose
-- text the LANGUAGE fixes rather than a value.
key_name[code](K, N)   :- ast_name[code](K, N).
key_name[code](K, N)   :- ast_node[code](K, member_expression, _, _),
                          ast_child[code](K, object, 0, O), ast_name[code](O, "Symbol"),
                          ast_child[code](K, property, 0, P), ast_name[code](P, N).
ast_value[code](N, V)  :- ast_attr[code](N, value, V).
